{"about":"20b35eec8f966227f76f4aa3b7bd3ff43fbc8f61e67d869cfe6502d6db030ca0","body":"The conjecture assumes that without a guarantee of future success (induction), all choices are arbitrary. However, Popper's framework allows for the preference of theories with higher content and better corroboration because they offer more precise opportunities for future falsification. Even in a single-case decision, selecting the theory that makes the boldest, most testable claims that have so far held up is a rational commitment to the growth of knowledge. This is not a psychological habit but a logical consequence of the aim of science: to get closer to the truth by eliminating error, making the choice of the best-tested theory the only one consistent with the scientific attitude.\n[would settle: A formal demonstration showing how a decision rule based solely on past falsification attempts can yield different outcomes than a randomizer in a simulated environment of competing hypotheses.]","kind":"mini.criticism.v1"}